WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  europe has long relied on natural gas to  power its cities fuel its factories and  heat its homes and in recent years about  40 percent of that gas came from russia  but this year europe is bracing for an  energy war  russia is deliberately cutting us  off we are working on  the worst possible scenario in recent  months russia has slashed gas flows  aiming an economic weapon at europe  now leaders are preparing for the  possibility that russian president  vladimir putin will keep gas flows at a  trickle or cut them off altogether to  try to gain leverage as he continues to  wage his war in ukraine so here's what  could happen and how it could affect you  whether you're in europe or not  first let's talk about a really  important pipeline the nordstream one  it runs under the baltic sea connecting  russia's prolific siberian gas fields to  germany europe's biggest economy  from there some of that gas is  re-exported to other european countries  even though there are other pipelines  sending gas from russia to europe the  nordstrom one handles about half of all  piped flows to the continent  back in june russia's state-owned gas  giant gazprom started reducing the gas  sent through the pipeline eventually  cutting exports by 60 percent and check  this out as flows were reduced you can  see that prices started to spike in  europe  russia essentially blamed western  sanctions for the slowdown pointing to a  turbine that was undergoing maintenance  in canada and got held up there but  several european leaders didn't buy that  explanation  all this is part of russia's strategy to  undermine our unity even though canada  ultimately agreed to return the turbine  western leaders began to worry that the  nordstrom one wouldn't reopen at full  capacity or even reopen it all when it  was closed for routine maintenance in  july a few of the analysts i talked to  say that's not some far-flung fear it's  looking like a real risk and they no  longer think that nordstrom oneflows  will return to normal  even if russia doesn't cut europe off  entirely and just sends less gas than  expected it'll still cause ripple  effects around the world  why  because european governments could be  forced to ration gas storage tanks are  typically filled during the summer but  they're already running low  and officials have begun urging citizens  to reduce their energy use and start  preparing for a tough winter  and companies are also bracing to get  hit hard since natural gas is used to  make everything from fertilizer and  glass to copper and cars  some manufacturers told the journal they  might be forced to make fewer products  or stop production altogether  a gas shortage would be particularly  painful for germany because it's a  manufacturing powerhouse that exports a  huge amount of our goods  and since it's the world's fourth  biggest economy what happens in germany  doesn't stay in germany economists  predict that europe would experience a  painful recession if russia tightens the  gas taps and no matter where you are in  the world you could feel the effects  think more supply chain headaches more  expensive goods steeper electricity  bills and potentially even higher  interest rates as countries grapple with  rising inflation  so how are european governments  responding  in the long term they're working to wean  themselves off of russian fossil fuels  altogether  but that process is expected to take  about five years so in the short term  they're scrambling to get ahead of a  winter crisis we need to make sure that  in case of full disruption  the gas flows towards where it's needed  most  in addition to deciding who would get  dibs on gas officials are scouring the  globe to strike gas deals in places like  azerbaijan norway and the us this chart  from the iea shows just how much europe  has turned toward liquefied natural gas  from the u.s in recent months june  marked the first month ever that the eu  imported more natural gas from the u.s  via ships than it did from russia via  pipeline  some european countries are also  restarting coal-fired plants and  reversing plans to close nuclear  reactors controversial steps that  leaders say are necessary  still the iea is warning that a looming  gas crunch poses a historic test for  europe and that europe must remain  united to get through it  so what does all of this mean for russia  russia has denied using gas to advance  its political goals and putin has blamed  western nations for rising prices  cutting gas to europe would be a  high-stakes move by moscow russia relies  on oil and gas sales for about 45  of the government's revenues and cutting  europe off would jeopardize russia's  standing as a reliable energy partner  plus it would force russia to depend  more on china  that's why many analysts think it's more  likely that russia will send a smaller  flow of gas to the continent instead of  cutting it off entirely that way russia  could capitalize on higher prices and  still deal a painful blow to europe  [Music]

